I have a streaming cache in C that evicts experts from RAM to NVMe. My eviction loop walks a doubly-linked list and it's showing up as 40% of CPU in perf. The list has ~13k entries. Here's the hot function:

static void s3fifo_touch(entry *e) {
  if (e->in_hot) return;
  list_remove(&hot, e);
  e->freq = e->freq > 2 ? 2 : e->freq + 1;
  list_push_front(&hot, e);
}

Why would a linked list be this slow and what's the fix?

Use a slab of entries with index links and split the lock by role. The producer only touches the hot queue tail; the evictor only touches the main queue and the ghost set. Give each queue its own spinlock and never hold both. The freq bump is a single relaxed atomic on a per-entry counter, no lock needed. Here is the node:

typedef struct entry {
  uint32_t next, prev;   // index into entries[], UINT32_MAX = nil
  uint8_t  freq;         // 0..2
  uint8_t  queue;        // 0=small 1=main 2=ghost
  uint32_t expert_id;
} entry;

The touch becomes O(1) with at most one cache miss when the entry is already hot. Want me to sketch the split-lock evict path next?
